WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.278
Sleep is best understood as a support condition for learning.

2
00:00:04.278 --> 00:00:07.700
It supports attention, memory, recovery, and performance stability.

3
00:00:07.700 --> 00:00:11.977
It does not replace instruction, feedback, retrieval, or deliberate practice.

4
00:00:11.977 --> 00:00:20.532
A person still has to engage with the material, use the skill, correct mistakes, and return to practice over time.

5
00:00:20.532 --> 00:00:26.093
Sleep helps create the conditions in which that work can happen more reliably.

6
00:00:26.093 --> 00:00:29.087
Learning is more than exposure to information.

7
00:00:29.087 --> 00:00:35.076
A learner can read, watch, or attend and still fail to build durable understanding.

8
00:00:35.076 --> 00:00:43.203
The brain has to select important information, process it, connect it to prior knowledge, and make it available later.

9
00:00:43.203 --> 00:00:54.752
Poor sleep can interfere with focus, judgment, emotional regulation, and consistency, which means the learner may spend time near the material without getting much learning from it.

10
00:00:54.752 --> 00:00:58.602
The difference between studying more and learning better matters.

11
00:00:58.602 --> 00:01:06.301
More hours are not automatically better if attention is weak, errors go unnoticed, or recall is never practiced.

12
00:01:06.301 --> 00:01:12.290
A well-designed learning system combines focused practice, useful feedback, review, spacing, and recovery.

13
00:01:12.290 --> 00:01:21.700
Sleep is one part of that system because tired learners often struggle to encode and use what they are trying to learn.

14
00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:24.695
This course stays practical and non-clinical.

15
00:01:24.695 --> 00:01:32.394
It does not diagnose sleep problems, offer treatment guidance, recommend medications, or replace qualified medical or psychological advice.

16
00:01:32.394 --> 00:01:45.654
The focus is learning design and workplace performance: how sleep and recovery affect readiness, how fatigue changes learning conditions, and how teams can build schedules and routines that respect human limits.
